# Self-improving loop trigger

When Wyatt's message begins with `loop:`, treat the remaining text as the immutable task brief.
The graph owns plan and checker evidence. No stage may edit source, commit, push, deploy, or claim
a player playtest PASS.

1. Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/BRIEFING.md`, and the top of `docs/STATUS.md`. Bind the one active
   valid card ID. If no card is active, or the brief names a different card, ask Wyatt to select or
   activate the correct card. Do not replace shared `prompt.md` or `log.md`.
2. Start the graph with the complete task brief on standard input:
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py start --card-id <active-card-id>`.
   Read the returned `run_id`. A dirty worktree, existing graph lock, or invalid start is a hard
   stop. Do not clean, stash, or retry.
3. Run the fixed maker bridge once:
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py run-maker --run-id <run-id>`.
   Continue only when the returned state is `await_checker` and contains matching `brief`,
   `baseline`, `plan`, `maker`, and `checker_request` artifacts. A blocked state is terminal.
4. Run exactly one fixed DeepSeek checker:
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py run-checker --run-id <run-id>`.
   The graph invokes the runner as `--role checker` with a fixed read-only tool set and a graph-bound
   run ID. It records `checker-receipt.json`, including the checker state/result digests and its
   parsed `APPROVE`, `REJECT`, or `ESCALATE` verdict. Do not invoke a second model or submit a
   replacement review by hand.
5. If the checker fails, times out, has a tool error, returns an invalid final decision, or the
   worktree/artifacts drift, the graph enters a terminal state and releases its lock. Do not retry
   the checker or reuse another run's result.
6. If the graph returns `await_ack`, show Wyatt the plan and checker receipt. Stop for the exact human
   acknowledgement `ack <card-id>`, then record it with
   `python .agent/bin/dev_graph.py ack --run-id <run-id> --text "ack <card-id>"`. The complete
   graph is terminal. Do not start Sol or an implementation maker run. Any later source apply is a
   separate normal repository action under the existing per-wave plan and ACK rules.

`ship it` is separate deployment authorization. Never print or request the API key in chat.
